🚀 𝗗𝗮𝘆 𝟮𝟳/𝟯𝟲𝟱 — 𝗗𝗲𝘃𝗢𝗽𝘀 𝗜𝗻𝘁𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗲𝘄 𝗖𝗵𝗮𝗹𝗹𝗲𝗻𝗴𝗲

🔥 𝗞𝘂𝗯𝗲𝗿𝗻𝗲𝘁𝗲𝘀 𝗦𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗰𝗲 𝗶𝘀 𝗦𝗹𝗼𝘄 — 𝗛𝗼𝘄 𝗪𝗶𝗹𝗹 𝗬𝗼𝘂 𝗧𝗿𝗼𝘂𝗯𝗹𝗲𝘀𝗵𝗼𝗼𝘁 𝗜𝘁?

𝗜𝗻𝘁𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗲𝘄 𝗤𝘂𝗲𝘀𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻:

👉 “Your Kubernetes application is running, but requests through the Service are very slow. How will you troubleshoot it?”

𝗜 𝘄𝗼𝘂𝗹𝗱𝗻’𝘁 𝗶𝗺𝗺𝗲𝗱𝗶𝗮𝘁𝗲𝗹𝘆 𝗿𝗲𝘀𝘁𝗮𝗿𝘁 𝘁𝗵𝗲 𝗽𝗼𝗱𝘀. First, I’d trace the request path.

🔍 𝗧𝗿𝗼𝘂𝗯𝗹𝗲𝘀𝗵𝗼𝗼𝘁𝗶𝗻𝗴 𝗙𝗹𝗼𝘄

1️⃣ Check Service & Endpoints

kubectl get svc -n <namespace>
kubectl get endpoints <service-name> -n <namespace>

Make sure the Service has the expected endpoints.

2️⃣ Check Pod Health

kubectl get pods -n <namespace>
kubectl top pods -n <namespace>

Check CPU, memory, restarts and readiness.

3️⃣ Test Service Internally

kubectl run test --rm -it --image=curlimages/curl -- \
curl http://<service-name>:<port>

This helps determine whether the latency exists inside the cluster.

4️⃣ Check Application Logs

kubectl logs <pod-name> -n <namespace>

Look for:

🔹 Timeouts
🔹 Retries
🔹 Database delays
🔹 External API latency
🔹 Connection errors

5️⃣ Check Dependencies

If pod resources are normal, investigate:

🔹 Database
🔹 Cache
🔹 Other microservices
🔹 External APIs
🔹 Network/DNS latency

6️⃣ Check Recent Changes

Look for recent:

🔹 Deployments
🔹 Configuration changes
🔹 Traffic increases
🔹 Infrastructure/network changes

🎯 𝗧𝗵𝗲 𝗙𝗹𝗼𝘄

Client → Ingress/LB → Service → Endpoint → Pod → Application → Dependencies

𝗞𝗲𝘆 𝗟𝗲𝗮𝗿𝗻𝗶𝗻𝗴:

A healthy Pod does not always mean a healthy request path.

Always isolate where the latency is introduced before taking recovery action.
